1.Frequency Measurement Method:
 The frequency meter uses the Equal-Precision Frequency Measurement Method to measure an unknown input frequency. Instead of counting only the input pulses over a fixed period, this method counts both the test clock and a reference clock during the same measurement interval.
The PYNQ-Z2's 125 MHz clock generates a 100 MHz reference clock using Vivado's Clocking Wizard. A measurement gate is generated and synchronized to the test clock. While the gate is active, two counters record the number of test-clock cycles (Nx) and reference-clock cycles (Ns).
Once the measurement is complete, the frequency is calculated using:
fx= NxNs fs
Where:
fx: Measured test frequency (Hz)
fs: Reference frequency (100 MHz)
Nx: Number of test-clock cycles
Ns: Number of reference-clock cycles
Because the measurement window in a conventional frequency counter may not align with the input signal, it can introduce a counting uncertainty of approximately ±1 pulse. This error becomes more significant when measuring lower frequencies.
Equal-precision measurement reduces this problem by synchronizing the measurement interval with the input clock; using a high frequency reference clock to determine the elapsed time.This approach improves measurement accuracy across a wider range of input frequencies.
