The frequency meter was tested using Vivado's behavioral simulation to observe the clock signals, measurement gate, and counter operation. The figure above shows the simulation waveform, including the system clock (`sys_clk`), test clock (`clk_test`), software gate (`gate_s`), and synchronized measurement gate (`gate_a`).

The waveform shows clock activity and changes in the gate signals. The simulation was limited to approximately 1 µs, which is shorter than the measurement interval used in the final design. As a result, the simulation does not demonstrate a completed frequency calculation.

8. FPGA Board Testing: 
The frequency meter was tested on the PYNQ-Z2 FPGA board using an internally generated test clock of approximately 12.987 MHz. The test clock was routed from output pin W9 to input pin A20 using a jumper wire. The measured frequency was displayed using the Digilent PmodSSD.

Since the PmodSSD can only display two digits at a time, multiple readings were recorded and grouped to identify a pattern to values for the measured frequency.

Collected Data 
12 98 70 13 
12 85 19 96 
12 98 70 13 
12 98 12 19 
12 98 70 12 
12 98 00 26 
12 98 78 29 
12 98 70 13 
The recurring sequence around 12 98 70 13 suggests a measured frequency of approximately 12.987013 MHz, compared with the expected 12.987 MHz. If these pairs represent one complete measurement, the difference is approximately 13 Hz.
To verify the meter's response, the test-clock jumper was disconnected. The display returned to zero, confirming that the displayed readings depended on the presence of the input signal.
Overall, the FPGA hardware test demonstrated that the frequency meter responds correctly to the test clock and produces readings consistent with the expected frequency. However, the exact measurement accuracy could not be independently verified from the two-digit display.



